      "In 1955 the American Museum of Natural History bought the former Painted Canyon Ranch with David Rockefeller's help and opened the Southwestern Research Station. Mont A. Cazier was the first director. Biologists, then birders, then dark-sky travelers turned a remote cattle bench into one of North America's best-known field sites, using the elevational transect from desert scrub to fir as a living laboratory. Fire, especially the 2011 Horseshoe Two burn of some 90,000 hectares, rewrote flood behavior in Cave Creek. Debris flows, closed trails, and a longer fire season are now part of the human calendar.",
