      "P'squosa (Wenatchi) people kept villages at the Icicle–Wenatchee forks and at the Columbia confluence, with first-foods ceremonies drawing Salish-speaking kin from a wide interior. Salmon, serviceberry, and a seasonal round up the tributaries structured the year. Fishing sites at the confluence were among the most important on the mid-Columbia. Treaties promised a reservation that was never fully delivered; many Wenatchi were pushed toward the Colville Reservation.",
      "The Great Northern Railway and federal irrigation on the Columbia turned the trench into apple country in the early twentieth century. High-head dams drowned fishing places and rewired the hydrograph. Irrigation districts mapped gravity and pumps onto bench lands that would otherwise be shrub-steppe. Wenatchee branded itself the Apple Capital; packing sheds and warehouses still explain the grid. Grand Coulee's power and water were the larger machine. The apple is an engineered native of this climate, not a wild one.",
      "Wildfire smoke, a warming river, and suburban spread onto orchard land are the present harvest. Packing, cold storage, and a seasonal labor force (including generations of Latino orchard families) are the human system. The town remains a service hub for a fruit empire that needs cold winters, careful frost fights, and labor. Fire in the Chelan and Entiat country sends smoke into the trench because inversions trap it. Climate here is a production input.",
